1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,370
Say on day one of the month you swipe a credit card for ten million dong of work gear at an equipment counter.

2
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:09,460
Thirty days later, your banking app pings with the bill.

3
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:17,680
Two payment choices sit side by side on your screen: pay all ten million dong in full, or pay the five-percent minimum of five hundred thousand dong.

4
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:28,660
Tapping five hundred thousand counts as paying on time, triggers zero late fees, protects your credit file, and keeps nine point five million dong sitting in checking for two more weeks until payday.

5
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:29,540
Painless, right?

6
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:35,390
Except when next month's bill lands on your phone, your balance did not shrink to nine point five million.

7
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:39,030
It jumped up to ten million ninety-two thousand five hundred dong.

8
00:00:39,030 --> 00:00:47,180
Hold on: you just wired five hundred thousand, so how did five hundred and ninety-two thousand five hundred dong in interest pile right back on top?

9
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:53,800
To catch that clock rewinding, let us rewind all the way back to Day One of the billing cycle.

10
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:57,770
You tap your card for ten million dong at that equipment counter.

11
00:00:57,770 --> 00:01:06,760
Within seconds, an authorization request races across card rails to your issuer, checks your limit, and locks ten million dong of available credit.

12
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:11,280
Notice that zero real cash moved out of your checking account at checkout.

13
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:22,650
If a small office bought that gear, its accountant simply logged ten million into expenses under Debit Account six forty-two and opened a payable under Credit Account three thirty-eight.

14
00:01:22,650 --> 00:01:29,810
Meanwhile, you walk out with the gear under the card's classic headline promise: up to forty-five interest-free days.

15
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:39,440
Cardholders naturally ask: if my statement is not even due until day forty-five, how can the bank possibly charge interest back to day one?

16
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:41,990
Look just two business days past checkout.

17
00:01:41,990 --> 00:01:46,610
That equipment shop is not waiting forty-five days to pay rent and salaries.

18
00:01:46,610 --> 00:02:03,130
By Day T plus two, your card-issuing bank already pulled real funds out of its treasury and wired nine million eight hundred and fifty thousand dong to the store's bank, leaving the shop with nine million eight hundred thousand after a two-percent merchant processing fee.

19
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:08,900
That means for more than a whole month, you were spending cash your bank fronted out of pocket.

20
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:16,610
They waive interest on those forty-five days under one strict bargain: clear one hundred percent of the bill on Day forty-five.

21
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:29,830
On day thirty, your card issuer freezes the monthly transaction list and delivers statement one, giving you fifteen more days until day forty-five to gather funds.

22
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:32,330
Two clear roads open up on screen.

23
00:02:32,330 --> 00:02:41,000
Road one: wire all ten million dong on or before day forty-five, and all forty-five days of bank money cost zero dong.

24
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,070
Road two: pay the five-percent minimum of five hundred thousand dong.

25
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:54,160
Apps place that small number right under your thumb to help borrowers dodge late penalties when cash is tight.

26
00:02:54,160 --> 00:03:04,450
What rarely jumps off the screen is the catch: tapping five hundred thousand forfeits all forty-five interest-free days you thought you had.

27
00:03:05,510 --> 00:03:11,630
On day forty-five, you send five hundred thousand dong from checking over to the card issuer.

28
00:03:11,630 --> 00:03:18,800
Real cash lands on time, knocking principal down from ten million to nine million five hundred thousand dong.

29
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:29,980
On a business ledger under Circular ninety-nine, Account three thirty-eight falls by five hundred thousand and Account one twelve checking drops by five hundred thousand.

30
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:36,950
Paying that minimum spares you late penalty fees and keeps an overdue stain off your credit bureau report.

31
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:47,730
Yet the second midnight passes on day forty-five with nine point five million still unpaid, the issuer's system strips away the interest-free concession altogether.

32
00:03:49,090 --> 00:03:53,280
Open statement two on day sixty to watch the calculator work.

33
00:03:53,280 --> 00:04:09,270
At an illustrative credit card rate of thirty-six percent a year — call it three percent a month, or zero point one percent a day on a thirty-day month — the bank does not start charging on nine point five million from day forty-six.

34
00:04:09,270 --> 00:04:13,050
It rewinds to Day One and computes two separate slices.

35
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:26,090
Slice one runs across all forty-five initial days when the full ten million sat unpaid: ten million times zero point one percent times forty-five equals four hundred and fifty thousand dong.

36
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:42,700
Slice two runs across the next fifteen days after your payout on the nine million five hundred thousand remaining: nine point five million times zero point one percent times fifteen equals one hundred and forty-two thousand five hundred dong.

37
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:49,430
Add both slices together and interest hits five hundred and ninety-two thousand five hundred dong.

38
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:55,450
Put those numbers side by side and the minimum-payment shock becomes clear.

39
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:59,500
You paid five hundred thousand dong out of pocket on day forty-five.

40
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:13,330
Fifteen days later, the bank billed five hundred and ninety-two thousand five hundred dong in finance charges onto your nine point five million principal, lifting the new balance to ten million ninety-two thousand five hundred dong.

41
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:18,810
You paid five hundred thousand and wound up ninety-two thousand five hundred deeper in debt.

42
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:31,500
Look at what you actually bought: delaying payment on nine point five million for fifteen extra days cost five hundred and ninety-two thousand five hundred dong — six point two four percent for half a month alone.

43
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:40,620
Keep letting that balance roll at three percent compounded monthly, and your effective annual interest rate runs near forty-two point six percent a year.

44
00:05:41,830 --> 00:05:48,050
Worse still, unpaid revolving debt spills onto everything else you buy with that plastic.

45
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:58,670
Whenever you clear one hundred percent of every statement, fresh grocery or fuel runs automatically earn their own forty-five-day interest-free cushion.

46
00:05:58,670 --> 00:06:08,590
Once you pay only the minimum on day forty-five and carry nine point five million into cycle two, that shield vanishes across the entire card.

47
00:06:08,590 --> 00:06:20,890
At most card issuers, any new coffee or supply run you swipe from day forty-six onward starts earning zero point one percent daily interest the very second you tap at checkout.

48
00:06:21,990 --> 00:06:26,760
Watch out for three cousin traps that catch careful spenders too.

49
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:46,190
First, tiny rounding errors: transfer nine million nine hundred ninety thousand dong on day forty-five and miss ten thousand by mistake, and failing the one-hundred-percent rule still triggers four hundred and fifty thousand dong in back-interest on the whole bill.

50
00:06:46,190 --> 00:06:59,610
Second, cash withdrawals from a credit card at a cash machine: those carry zero grace days, hit you with roughly a four-percent upfront fee, and accrue daily interest from second one.

51
00:06:59,610 --> 00:07:14,790
Third, trailing interest: even when you pay all ten million ninety-two thousand five hundred on day sixty-five, those five days since statement cutoff still plant stray daily interest onto next month's bill.

52
00:07:16,300 --> 00:07:28,450
To be fair, the five-percent minimum button is not a scam; it is an emergency shock absorber built to shield your credit history and block late fees when cash runs short.

53
00:07:28,450 --> 00:07:34,030
In return, you pay full rent on the cash the bank fronted for forty-five days.

54
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:54,040
Next time you open your banking app and see a gentle five-hundred-thousand minimum option sitting under a ten-million card bill, run that quick check in your head: pay five hundred thousand today, and five hundred and ninety-two thousand five hundred in interest lands next month.

55
00:07:54,040 --> 00:08:00,330
Only paying one hundred percent of the statement brings those forty-five free days back.
